        "title": "What do you know about the food you eat?",
        "text": "A  Most of us don't really think about what we eat. Of course, we may have our favourite recipes, or worry about whether our food has been sprayed with pesticides, but the processes and discoveries that have gone into producing it remain a closed book. Some people, however, think differently. Why, they wonder, is frozen milk yellow? Why does your mouth burn for longer when you eat chillies than when you eat mustard? And what would happen if you threw yourself into a swimming pool full of jelly?\n\nB  It was for people like these that New Scientist developed its 'Last Word' column, in which readers ask, and answer, questions on all kinds of obscure scientific issues as they relate to everyday life. Many of the questions raised have simple answers. For the ones above, they would be: the riboflavin in milk begins to crystallise; it depends on your taste, because the relevant chemical in mustard is more easily washed away by your saliva; and you would float, but don't dive in head first!\n\nC  Other questions let us explore issues that matter to everyone. For example, what is the difference between sell-by dates and use-by dates? You might expect the answer to involve over-cautious health and safety rules, but it is more complicated than that. The shelf life of food is actually decided by its manufacturers, although lab tests and government guidelines also play a part. Food is tested regularly, at various temperatures, to check the level of bacterial spoilage over a few hours or days: the warmer it is, the more likely your prawn sandwich is to make you ill. After the lab tests, producers set a use-by date or a best-before date. Fresh shellfish must be eaten by their use-by date (the date by which you must eat them). But tinned beans will probably last long after their best-before date (the date by which it is best to eat them), although they may not taste as good as they once did.\n\nD  The same research explains why even bottled mineral water, which had been lying underground for decades, needs a best-before date. The problem is not the water but the bottling process: either bacteria can get in, multiply and, over time, contaminate the water, or unpleasant chemicals, such as antimony, can leach into the water from the plastic bottles.\n\nE  Sometimes this kind of scientific study takes us to some strange places. For example, we now know that the amount of oxygen in the air inside green peppers is higher than in red ones (by a whopping 1.23 per cent), probably because green peppers photosynthesise at a different rate. The point of this research is that green peppers will decay faster than red ones if they are kept in sunlight: higher oxygen levels provide more resources to feed any bacteria that are present. In general, food keeps best in cooler places, except for tropical fruit. Banana skins, for example, have evolved to survive in warm conditions, because that is where bananas grow best. Any temperature below 13.3°C damages the membranes, releasing enzymes which make the skin go black. To avoid a mushy banana, keep it out of the fridge.\n\nF  It is not only fears about our health that keep food scientists busy; they work in other areas too. Their precision has also been applied to bottles, for example, and in particular to the discovery that the best number of sharp pointy bits on a bottle cap is 21. Go on, count them. Years of trial and error led to the internationally accepted German standard DIN 6099, which ensures that almost every bottle cap is the same. This is because 21 is the ideal number when you take into account the circumference of the cap, the likelihood of its metal splitting, and the chances of it sticking in the capping machine. So, next time you open a bottle with a cap on it, pay tribute to the people who bothered to find out, starting with William Painter in 1892.\n\nG  Of course, some researchers do care about the more serious issues, driven by fear of the future and an ever-growing population on a warming planet that is short of land. Sadly, New Scientist's correspondents concluded that there was no single food that could feed the world on its own. However, they did come up with a menu that could feed a family of four for 365 days a year, using only eight square metres of land. Rotating crops (so that the soil did not lose any one nutrient more than the others) would be vital, as would ploughing back dead plant matter and keeping to a vegetarian diet. After that, you would need to grow crops that take up very little space and, if possible, grow vertically rather than horizontally."
